# How to run

./run.sh --skill-file task/SKILL.md

# What a run leaves behind

    runs/<session>/run.log          full trace - see below
    runs/<session>/transcript.jsonl the agent loop as JSON, one event per line
    runs/<session>/steps.log        one line per pipeline milestone, with cost
    runs/<session>/tool_output/     any tool result too big to show the model

`run.log` is the one to read when a run went wrong. It is plain text, one
timestamped line per event, and it is a superset of what scrolled past on the
terminal:

    2026-08-18 19:55:58.025 | +    2.0s | INFO  | turn      | 2 tool call(s) names=bash,read_file
    2026-08-18 19:55:58.044 | +    2.0s | INFO  | bash      | exit=0 secs=0.02 out_chars=396 err_chars=0

Multi-line payloads follow their line indented with `|`, so `grep '^20'` gives
you the event timeline and `grep -A50 'reasoning | turn 7'` gives you the
thinking behind turn 7. Columns are timestamp, elapsed, level, component,
message - and components are worth knowing: `http` (every request, its latency
and its token usage), `turn`, `reasoning`, `tool`, `bash`, `vision`, `approval`,
`context` (compactions), `step0`, `console` (anything printed).

Three things it holds that nothing else does. The model's chain of thought,
which is dropped from the conversation after each turn and otherwise only ever
seen 600 characters at a time under `-v`. Every message exactly as it was sent
to the server, logged once when it first appears and again if compaction
rewrites it. And step 0's own output, which used to go straight to the terminal
and nowhere else.

Secrets and base64 image payloads are stripped on the way in, so a trace is safe
to paste. Flags:

    --trace-level INFO     drop the reasoning and the HTTP payloads
    --no-trace             do not write it at all

# Step 0 - the reference is picked for you

Every run starts by cleaning the source and then choosing the lay reference,
deterministically, before the agent gets a turn:

    tools/clean.py              erases the tag, drops the background and plates
                                it white, writing
                                runs/<session>/archive/offset_upload.jpg
    tools/match_reference.py    scores all of library_reference/ against THAT
                                cleaned image and returns ONE winner or an
                                honest "no match"
    tools/select_reference.py   installs that winner as
                                inputs/reference_greyscale.jpg (desaturated,
                                full resolution) and records where it came from

Cleaning runs first because every library image is a garment on a white plate.
Matching the raw phone photo against them scores the room and the hang tag as
part of the garment, and the number that comes out is what the whole run is
anchored to. The work is not extra: prepare.py used to do exactly this on the
agent's first turn and now verifies the result instead of redoing it, so there
is one cleaning path and the spend is unchanged.

If cleaning fails the run continues against the raw input, prints
`WARNING: matched against raw input`, and records `query_cleaned: false` in the
receipt - degraded, not dead.

harness.py runs the pair itself. `library_reference/` is kept out of the
workspace inventory so the agent never sees a second candidate to argue with,
and the run folder gets:

    runs/<session>/reference_selection.json   source file, score, runner-up,
                                              what still differs, and which
                                              image was matched (path, md5,
                                              query_cleaned)
    runs/<session>/match_results.json         every attribute record and score
    runs/<session>/result_top_matches.jpg     query beside the top 5

**No match stops the run before the agent starts** - a laydown measured against
the wrong garment's reference is worse than no laydown. Flags:

    --no-reference-select        use whatever reference inputs/ already holds
    --no-pre-clean               match against the raw input; prepare.py then
                                 cleans on the agent's first turn as before
    --reference-category bras    force the library subfolder
    --reference-threshold 85     lower the pass mark (default 87)
    --allow-no-reference         start anyway, with no match

Or run it by hand:

    cd tools && ../.venv/bin/python select_reference.py --dry-run
    cd tools && ../.venv/bin/python match_reference.py --list-categories

# What step 0 needs

A vision model on an OpenAI-compatible endpoint - NOT the same server the
harness uses for its own text model:

    REFMATCH_BASE_URL   default http://10.11.243.169:8080/v1  (include /v1)
    .qwen_key           the API key, or QWEN_API_KEY in the environment

Deliberately not QWEN_BASE_URL: harness.py reads that one for its text model and
appends /v1 itself, so sharing the variable pointed one of the two at the wrong
path.

The attribute cache lives in `.cache/refmatch/`. Cold it costs ~115s for 45
library images; warm, a run is a few seconds. Deleting it can shift borderline
scores, because the model re-describes each image - so it is worth keeping.
